      <workList xml:id="workDesc_a9988a8f">
         <work xml:id="work_id863efd49">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_5431dbaa">C4:009</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id0c04ba7c" xml:lang="de">Moralische Fabeln mit beygefügten Erklärungen einer jeden Fabel</title>
            <title type="original" xml:id="title_390a80f3" xml:lang="da">Moralske Fabler med hosføyede Forklaringer til hver Fabel</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_8a2602d5">
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_idee68aaff">Ludvig Holberg</persName>
               <persName role="translator" xml:id="persName_eff6b32e">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_idceb7831a">
               <date isodate="1751" xml:id="date_49031ea6">1751</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_idd6d66c08">
               <p xml:id="idm195">Having just moved to Sønderborg, Scheibe was commissioned to translate <persName xml:id="idm196" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Ludvig Holberg</persName>’s <rend xml:id="idm197" fontstyle="italic">Moralske Fabler</rend> of 1751. He was well-acquainted with Holberg, the professor at the university and playwright who was also an able musical amateur. The previous year he had been working on Holberg’s <rend xml:id="idm198" fontstyle="italic">Peder Paars</rend> (SchW C4:011). Only a few weeks after the Danish edition of <rend xml:id="idm199" fontstyle="italic">Moralske Fabler</rend> had appeared, Scheibe was asked to make a German translation – both publishers and Scheibe thought it a good idea to issue a translation for the German audience. The translation must have been finished in March 1751, for Scheibe dated his foreword 13 March. Furthermore, for various reasons Scheibe only had fourteen days to carry out the translation. As became a habit in his later translations, Scheibe included a foreword explaining his approach to the translation, and finally he lists some errors of his previous translation (<rend xml:id="idm200" fontstyle="italic">Peter Paars</rend>; C4:011) which needed to be emended. The edition was apparently so popular that it was issued again the following years, 1752, in 1761 and finally in 1769 though without changes. It should be noted that the publishers issued a new and ‘improved’ edition in 1767, translated by <persName xml:id="idm201" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Elias Caspar Reichard</persName>. Scheibe's translation of 1751 was reviewed by <persName xml:id="idm202" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</persName> in <rend xml:id="idm203" fontstyle="italic">Vossische Zeitung.</rend> However, the 1767-edition by Reichard received a rather negative review in <rend xml:id="idm204" fontstyle="italic">Allgemeine deutsche Bibliothek</rend> written by Lessing's brother, <persName xml:id="idm205" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Karl Gotthelf</persName>. Perhaps this was the reason why the publishers chose to reprint Scheibe’s translation in 1769.</p>
            </history>
